# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PitchTerminal V2 — backend environment
#
# ADDITIVE. V1 keeps using .env exactly as before; nothing here is required by
# any V1 job, and src/config/index.ts is not aware of these variables. A process
# that runs only V1 work needs none of them.
#
# Copy the lines you need into .env. NEVER commit real credentials.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Connection ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Direct host is db.<project>.supabase.co (IPv6 only without the IPv4 add-on).
# Shared pooler is aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com (IPv4, needs the suffix below).
PT_V2_DB_HOST=db.<project>.supabase.co
PT_V2_DB_NAME=postgres

# SESSION MODE ONLY. 6543 is the transaction pooler and is refused outright.
# 5432 is session mode on BOTH the direct host and the shared pooler host.
#
# R-58: pipeline connections operate in session mode because bulk write paths
# depend on session-scoped state — the retention marker of R-21 and the timeout
# settings of A.15 — that transaction pooling does not preserve. A pooled
# connection does not fail loudly: the marker would not survive to the next
# statement, the DELETE policy would match nothing, and retention would report
# success having removed nothing.
PT_V2_DB_PORT=5432

PT_V2_DB_SSL=true

# 30000, RAISED FROM 10000 ON MEASUREMENT. A staged handshake trace against the
# deployed Supabase pooler showed the network handshake completing in ~3.3s and
# the FULL connection — pooler authentication plus its own connection to the
# tenant database — taking 14.1s on a cold tenant. A 10s budget killed every
# attempt before it could finish, so retrying could never help. 30000 is roughly
# twice the observed worst case.
#
# IF YOUR .env PINS THIS AT 10000, RAISE IT. A value in the file overrides the
# default, and `npm run doctor:v2` now warns when it is below the floor.
PT_V2_DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
PT_V2_DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# application_name, so pg_stat_activity attributes every session and the
# connection budget of R-05 is observable rather than inferred.
PT_V2_APP_NAME=pitchterminal-v2

# ── The database login ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE connection, ONE credential — the way V1 operates. Nothing about running V2
# requires provisioning a PT-specific database identity.
#
# PT_V2_DB_USER defaults to `postgres`, the login a Supabase project already has
# and whose password the operator already holds. Setting it is only necessary to
# run the application under a narrower login.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, STATED SO IT IS A CHOICE: on Supabase the `postgres` role
# typically carries BYPASSRLS, so row-level policies are inert for THIS
# connection. Policies governing `anon` and `authenticated` are unaffected — the
# frontend does not connect as this role — so the requirement that the football
# layer carry explicit, uniform RLS (doc 04 B8 #33) still holds. `doctor:v2`
# reports which login is in force.
# PT_V2_DB_USER=postgres
#
# SET THE BASE LOGIN ONLY. A provider dashboard shows the JOINED form
# (`postgres.<project-ref>`) for pooler connections. Put the base name here and
# the tenant in PT_V2_DB_USER_SUFFIX below — the two are joined once at
# connection time. Pasting the joined form is tolerated (the tenant is stripped
# before it is re-applied) but the explicit pair is the intended model.

# DEFINE THIS EXACTLY ONCE. Within one file dotenv keeps the LAST definition, so
# a second one silently overrides the first and the earlier line is dead.
PT_V2_DB_PASSWORD=

# ── QUOTE EVERY PASSWORD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dotenv truncates an UNQUOTED value at the first `#`, silently and without a
# warning. A password of `Qx7pLm#4vZt2Rw9s` written unquoted arrives at the
# server as `Qx7pLm`, and the server answers `password authentication failed` —
# the same message it gives for a genuinely wrong password. Single quotes are
# the safe form: they preserve `#`, `$` and backslashes exactly.
#
#   PT_V2_DB_PASSWORD='Qx7pLm#4vZt2Rw9s'     correct
#   PT_V2_DB_PASSWORD=Qx7pLm#4vZt2Rw9s       silently becomes Qx7pLm
#
# `npm run doctor:v2` compares the file against what the process actually holds
# and names any variable where they differ.

# ── Shared pooler (only when the host is a pooler) ────────────────────────────
# A shared pooler multiplexes many databases behind one hostname and routes on a
# tenant identifier carried in the username, as `<user>.<suffix>`. Without it the
# pooler answers `ENOIDENTIFIER no tenant identifier provided`.
#
# This is CONNECTION ROUTING, not identity: PostgreSQL still authenticates and
# reports the bare login name.
#
# LEAVE EMPTY for a direct connection — a direct host has no tenant, and a suffix
# there produces a login name matching no role.
PT_V2_DB_USER_SUFFIX=

# ── TLS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Verification is ON by default and stays on.
#
# A managed provider signs with its OWN certificate authority, which the system
# trust store does not carry, so a first connection fails with:
#
#     self-signed certificate in certificate chain
#
# THE FIX IS THE BUNDLE, NOT A BYPASS. Supabase: Project Settings > Database >
# SSL Configuration > Download certificate. Point PT_V2_DB_SSL_CA at the .crt.
# A relative path resolves against the working directory, `~` is expanded, and
# the variable may hold the PEM text itself for a CI system that injects
# certificates as environment variables rather than files.
#
# `npm run doctor:v2` reports whether the bundle loaded and how many
# certificates it contains, and names the file when it could not be read.
# PT_V2_DB_SSL_CA=prod-ca-2021.crt
#
# Leave this alone. Disabling verification leaves the connection encrypted but
# unauthenticated, and the credential is the one thing on the wire worth
# stealing.
# PT_V2_DB_SSL_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=true

# ── Provider (S-4 ingestion only) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required by `npm run ingest:v2`. NOT required by `npm run seed:v2` or
# `npm run feature:v2`, neither of which imports the provider client.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT V1's SPORTSAPI_* VARIABLES, and nothing falls back to them.
# V2 must be deployable without inheriting V1 environment; a fallback would make
# a host holding only V1 secrets look configured while pointing V2 at whatever
# V1 happened to have. The values may well be the same secrets — that is a
# deployment's choice, made by setting both, not an inheritance the code
# performs on its own.
#
# The provider CODE is not configurable. SPORTSAPI_API is a source constant
# because it participates in the provider-scoped uniqueness constraints; a
# deployment able to change it would silently double every team, player,
# competition and fixture.
PT_V2_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=https://v2.football.sportsapipro.com/api
PT_V2_PROVIDER_KEY=

# Optional. A second key doubles the daily budget, 100 -> 200 requests.
PT_V2_PROVIDER_KEY_2=

# Optional, with defaults. MIN_INTERVAL_MS is 2000 because the squad endpoint
# requires two seconds between requests.
# PT_V2_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# PT_V2_PROVIDER_DAILY_QUOTA=100
# PT_V2_PROVIDER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=2000

# ── Pool sizing (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE pool, default 10. Session-mode connections are not multiplexed and Phase 8
# R-05 registers slot exhaustion as a High risk, so keep this modest.
#
# MINIMUM 2. withRun() holds a second connection for the job lifecycle, which
# must commit outside the work transaction so a failed run still leaves a record.
#
# PT_V2_POOL_MAX=10


# ── Local development / CI only ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Permits a non-standard port. NEVER set this in a deployed environment: it
# disables the one check that catches a pipeline pointed at a pooler.
# PT_V2_ALLOW_NON_SESSION_PORT=false
